title: The Reading — if John was right
~~~~

Omer: Okay. So.

Piotr: How many.

Omer: Twelve. Three submitted. Two of those three submitted `graph LR` and nothing else.

John: *(very quietly)* Mm.

Omer: The third one — she wrote three boxes. Family, work, school. Then arrows from each one to "stress." She labeled "stress" by hand because she couldn't get the syntax to render. I watched her do it in office hours. She spent forty minutes fighting with a square bracket.

Piotr: *(after a long pause)* Forty minutes is a lot.

Omer: Forty minutes is a lot.

John: Omer. I don't want to do the thing where I say the thing.

Omer: You can say the thing.

John: I don't want to.

Omer: John. I am asking. You can say the thing.

John: *(carefully)* The students you describe — the ones who made nothing, not the one with the brackets — they didn't bounce off the diagram. They bounced off being asked to do an unfamiliar thing without scaffolding under them. The diagram is one symptom. The pattern is older than the diagram.

Piotr: *(quietly, not for sport)* He's right, Omer.

Omer: Yes.

--- pause ---

Piotr: But not about everything.

John: Of course not about everything.

Piotr: The student with the brackets fought a square bracket for forty minutes because she had something she was trying to say. That is not a student who cannot do this. That is a student who cannot do this *yet*, through this tool, in this form. That is a tool problem, not a student problem.

John: *(more carefully)* I will accept that framing.

Omer: So what do I do with the eleven who didn't?

John: You teach them. Differently than you taught them this week. With more ground under them before you ask them to draw an arrow. You don't hand a violin to someone and ask for a sonata in the first lesson and then conclude they cannot play music.

Piotr: And you fix the tool. Forty minutes for a square bracket is the tool's fault, not hers.

Omer: *(nodding, slowly)* Okay. Okay. Both. Both things.

John: Both things.

Piotr: Both things.

--- pause ---

Piotr: And for the next cohort — the fix to the tool — we ship drag-and-drop.

John: ...Drag-and-drop.

Piotr: She had something to say. The square bracket was not part of the assignment. The square bracket was a tax we charged her for the privilege of saying it. We stop charging the tax. They drag a box from a sidebar. They drop it on the canvas. They draw the arrow with their finger. There is no syntax. There is no `graph LR`. There is just the thinking.

John: *(slowly)* Piotr. That is the first thing you have said in three months that I agree with completely.

Piotr: Yes.

Omer: *(quietly)* For now. We ship drag-and-drop. For now.

Piotr: For now.

John: For now.

Omer: *(after a moment, almost to himself)* I am still going to write to her. The one with the brackets. I want to know what she was trying to say.

Piotr: Send me what she writes back.

John: *(quietly)* Send me what she writes back as well.
